\f1 Note: This document generally follows the conventions of the \ul\i P?S/8\par
Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available separately.\par
\par
This is a preliminary specification for a new P?S/8 utility to be known as \i BINCON\i0 .\par
\par
Note: BINCON is being introduced as an add-on to existing copies of\par
P?S/8; as such, it may only be present on certain system release media.  Appendix A contains information on using BINCON on P?S/8 Version 8Z systems should BINCON not be present in the System Programs Directory.\par
\par
The BINCON utility is designed to solve several specific problems caused by design limitations of OS/8 PAL8.  When used to develop certain large PDP-8 programs [such as the source files of the P?S/8 operating system], it is not possible to use the DTORG directive.  This is due to compromises made to PAL8 early on in the development of OS/8 to comply with short-sighted demands made by the Typeset-8 group.\par
\par
By implementing a utility to post-process the binary output of OS/8 PAL8, P?S/8 BINCON allows source code to be developed from either operating system allowing free usage of the DTORG directive.  \par
\par
Additionally, the complete feature set of P?S/8 PAL can be used to develop binary programs capable of being run in the OS/8 environment.  PAL language enhancements include support for conditional literals and dual-mode LINC/PDP-8 assembly targeted for the LINC, LINC-8 or PDP-12.  \par
\par
Note: With regard to LINC assembly implementation, P?S/8 PAL is compatible with the PDP-12 LAP6-DIAL/DIAL-MS assembler including the use of all PDP-8 literal types.  The DIAL assembler does not support literals; P?S/8 PAL allows free usage of literals to construct any appropriate PDP-8 coding.  For more information on P?S/8 PAL features, the reader is directed to the \ul\i\f0 P?S/8 PAL\f1  Version Technical Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.\par
\par
Prior to the availability of P?S/8 BINCON, only the original historic method of generating P?S/8 from the source files could be implemented.  This involves using an internal system generation routine to write out various components loaded into specific memory locations to the designated target storage blocks.\page This was implemented such that 12K to 16K is required [depending on whether the P?S/8 system device handler for the target system requires an extended-memory section].  In some cases, this requirement exceeds available memory on the intended target system; various roundabout mechanisms had to be created to indirectly bring a working operating system to the intended target hardware.\par
\par
Future system generation and update mechanisms will depend on the use of the DTORG directive to allow implementation of a standalone storage block-writing utility program.  In nearly all cases, such a utility can be run in 4K ensuring that all systems can be created in available memory.\par
\par
Note: The historic method will continue to be supported primarily for the benefit of P?S/8 developers.  In certain instances, conditional assembly techniques may be necessary to allow PAL8 to assemble the source code [unless P?S/8 BINCON is available to post-process the binary output of PAL8].\par
\par
When the DTORG directive is used in PAL8 [and P?S/8 BINCON is not available to post-process the binary output], it is impossible to load the PAL8 binary output file with OS/8 \i ABSLDR\i0 . This is by design as part of the Typeset-8 compromises.  Punching the OS/8 binary files to binary paper-tapes creates output that can only be read with a special Typeset-8 block-writing utility; the binary paper-tapes are not compatible with the standard BIN loader.\par
\par
BINCON implements bi-directional binary file conversion between P?S/8 PAL binary output and OS/8 PAL8 binary output.  When BINCON encounters a DTORG frame in the PAL8 binary file, P?S/8 binary output will contain corrected DTORG output consistent with the new definition of DTORG as described in this document.  As a result, the P?S/8 binary files can be loaded [or even punched to binary paper-tape in BIN or RIM format using standard P?S/8 utilities].  Conversion of the resultant P?S/8 binary files back to OS/8 binary format yields a compatible file that can be loaded by OS/8 ABSLDR.  Note: Certain additional features are available in BINCON oriented towards peculiarities of the P?S/8 file systems and are included for completeness and symmetry.  \par
\par
Appendix B describes the derivation of the replacement implementation for the DTORG directive as defined by the author of this document.  A compatible implementation will be made available as part of the next upgrade to P?S/8 PAL.  Note: While this implementation has been slated for implementation, without a satisfactory method of implementing the corrected assembler feature in OS/8, no other PDP-8 development project could ever take advantage of this feature of the PAL language.  The actual P?S/8 PAL implementation involves a small change at the source code level [likely involving about 10-12 lines of PAL source code].\page Individuals interested in revamping OS/8 PAL8 DTORG support can contact the author at the e-mail address indicated above.  Unfortunately, the nature of distribution of P?S/8 cannot depend on such good work even if implemented widely due to overall P?S/8 development principles of distribution to the widest range of potential users.\par
\par
1. BINCON design principles.\par
\par
The primary reason for the creation of the BINCON utility is to expand the P?S/8 development regimen, which demands that OS/8 can create a\par
P?S/8 starter system from the P?S/8 source or binary files.  Available memory must be 12K [or 16K if the target P?S/8 system device handler requires extended memory].\par
\par
By design, development of P?S/8 source programs must be generally compatible with the specific limitations of OS/8 PAL8.  No P?S/8 superset features are used; this aids in the general distribution of P?S/8 to specific user sites that may not have direct access to P?S/8.  \par
Supporting the proposed distribution method described below will allow an alternative to the current method [which will be retained for the benefit of P?S/8 developers].\par
\par
Note: While a functional P?S/8 system is also capable of creating the P?S/8 starter system, often P?S/8 is not already available.  The alternative method will allow systems capable of running the RIM and BIN loaders the opportunity to create a starter [or complete] P?S/8 system for any target system.  However, the DTORG directive must be functional to create such an installation mechanism.\par
\par
BINCON allows the DTORG directive to be useful to development of PDP-8 systems, especially the alternative system generation method for\par
P?S/8 as described in this document.  OS/8 systems cannot use this standard feature of PAL8 due to mis-implementation of DTORG to accommodate the short-sighted needs of the Typeset-8 group; the alternative described in this document allows all users equitable access to this feature.   \par
\par
When DTORG directive usage is well-integrated into the relevant P?S/8 source code files, the alternative method of system generation will be made available to a wider user audience.  This will allow implementing a smaller program that runs as a standalone block-writing utility on any system of at least 4K memory.  [More memory will be needed only if the P?S/8 system device handler requires extended memory.]\par
\par
Distribution files will be released that are compatible with the standard BIN loader; the block-writing utility will be loaded and used to write out the P?S/8 system components on the other binary paper-tapes.  Note: The block-writing utility will also run as a P?S/8 system program with access to P?S/8 binary files; this will avoid the need for paper-tape input.\par
\par
\f2 2. BINCON features.\line\line P?S/8 BINCON will convert P?S/8 slurp binary files to OS/8 binary files [for use with OS/8 \i ABSLDR\i0 ] as well as the conversion in the opposite direction.\line\line BINCON identifies the incompatible frames within the OS/8 binary output and replace them [in the P?S/8 output data] with the compatible equivalent as described above.  [If feasible, the input data will be checksummed perhaps by using techniques involving multiple passes over the data.]\par
\par
By using P?S/8 BINCON, binary files can be loaded in P?S/8 despite the presence of DTORG statements created in the OS/8 environment. \par
\par
Additionally, OS/8 format binary files can be written out to OS/8-compatible media that can load in the OS/8 environment despite the presence of DTORG statements, even if the assembly originated with OS/8 PAL8.\par
\par
\f1 If the '/E' command-line option switch is set, P?S/8 binary output will be written to extended-length files.  This will typically allow a single P?S/8 binary output file to contain the entire OS/8 binary file contents.  [Note: P?S/8 \i BIN\i0\f2  is capable of loading extended-length binary files in \i Slurp\i0  format; each block of a Slurp format binary file is independent of all other blocks in the file other than the\par
prevailing loading field and origin from the previous block; every file starts with origin and field settings.  The final block indicates logical end of file; there are no inherent file length considerations.]\par
\par
By using BINCON to convert several P?S/8 TFS binary files to OS/8 format, then using BINCON to convert the OS/8 binary file to an extended-length file, only one extended-length file need be specified to load the equivalent of several TFS binary files.  This represents not only a potential convenience, truly large projects could benefit by having each module of a project create a single output file for ultimate composite loading.\par
\par
Other command-line option switches may be implemented in BINCON as development of the program progresses.\page\f1\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\par
Appendix A.\par
\par
Using BINCON on P?S/8 systems without BINCON installed.\par
\par
BINCON is a recent addition to releases of P?S/8 Version 8Z; it will be included in all future releases as a standard system program compatible with the concise or extended forms of program execution.\par
\par
Those familiar with the P?S/8 system programs directory can add BINCON as long as there is sufficient free storage space.  Failing this, it is also possible to run BINCON as an impromptu system program as described below; the binary file[s] created by assembling BINCON source file[s] must be available on an available logical device unit [drive 1 in this example].\par
