\par
BINCON is designed to be compatible with the work-around mechanism described above to allow rapid addition to existing systems; this mechanism was introduced in P?S/8 Version 8Y and is supported on all newer releases.\par
\par
Usage restrictions.\par
\par
There are no usage restriction associated with BINCON.  The program does not use the =\i xxxx\i0  construction; there is no internal overlay structure within the program.  The program is designed to start at 00200 to be compatible with the default program execution mechanism.\par
\par
End of Appendix A.\page\f1\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\par
Appendix B.\par
\par
History of the implementation of the DTORG directive and a new compatible replacement.\par
\par
\f1 1. Early implementation of DTORG for the DEC Typeset-8 group.\par
\par
On systems based on logical blocks of 128 words per block [such as The R-L Monitor System, POLY BASIC and related derivative operating systems, P?S/8 and Typeset-8] it is useful to designate specific logical blocks to write out particular operating system components.\par
\par
For example, the P?S/8 system device handler and integrated boot-up block must be written to logical block 0000 of the target operating system being created.\line\line Presently, this is accomplished by a large system generation utility that requires 12K-16K [depending on whether the system device handler requires an extended-memory section] as part of a complete routine to create a bootable starter system.  This memory requirement may exceed the available memory on the intended target system; elaborate workarounds must be contrived presently to create these systems.\par
\par
If each target block can be designated as part of a stand-alone block installation program, it is likely that all systems can be created on the actual intended hardware regardless of memory size.  [The only requirement would be that a suitable non-system device handler was available that can fit into the available memory size; this would usually be the case even if the target machine was 4K.]\line\line Typeset-8 takes advantage of the DTORG directive to implement this method of system generation on 4K Typeset-8 systems.  However, due to specific implementation limitations [that are arguably needless] the prevailing assemblers cannot be used to benefit development of any other operating system.\line\line The reason for this is that Typeset-8 is created from binary paper-tapes that are deliberately incompatible with all binary standards including the RIM and BIN loaders.\line\line The BIN file format does not support the DTORG directive output as implemented for Typeset-8.  An unsupported frame type is used to start the data intended to be written out on the block described by the DTORG frame pair.  [Note: The end of the data to be written is defined as all data prior to an origin setting outside of the current page as defined within the assembly; this part of the design is rational and quite acceptable.]\page Due to the presence of the unsupportable frame, it is impossible for the binary paper-tape to pass a checksum test as defined in the BIN loader specification.  As such, only the Typeset-8 program could confirm the validity of the data due to the incompatible format.\par
\par
Note: The previous sentence above is pure speculation; it may not be possible to confirm the validity or at the least it would require the paper-tape be read more than once, which is onerous.  Clearly, they never investigated this notion and merely ignored validation.\par
\par
The Typeset-8 block-writing utility program doesn't confirm validity of the paper-tape, merely writing out each data block as encountered.  \par
\par
Note: Many Typeset-8 systems were created using the low-speed reader on Teletype Model 33 ASR or 35 ASR terminals; these devices are known for reliability problems unless maintained an a schedule that is beyond impractical.  Undoubtedly, Typeset-8 development suffered some setbacks due to this rather poor design, occasionally writing out incorrect and unintended data.\par
\par
When Typeset-8 development meant much to DEC management, the Typeset-8 group was able to convince the developers of TOPS-10 PAL10 to support the distorted binary output format.  Very long paper-tapes were created and hand-walked to Typeset-8 machines to be read into memory [perhaps in some cases for hours at a time] as part of their rather disorganized attempt at software development.  Since TOPS-10 has no built-in requirement for compatibility with the PDP-8 BIN loader, this mis-feature was easily added to PAL10.  Unfortunately, expediency was the dominant factor in adding DTORG to PAL10, not the needs of the general user audience for unrelated program development.\par
\par
Note: It appears that at some point, DEC management wasn't even aware there were PDP-8 programming experts available outside of the meager number of people working on in-house development systems at the time.  When it became clear many "outsiders" existed, some being hired by DEC, no one ever revisited this feature and became essentially moribund.  Part of the problem is there is no direct benefit to OS/8 development to use the DTORG feature.  Yet the methods described here using DTORG also apply to other DEC products.  [About the only products the concept doesn't apply to are the Disk Monitor System and OS/8.]\par
\par
However, this issue had come up even before the OS/8 project was contemplated:\par
\par
POLY BASIC was developed [well over 95%] at Brooklyn Poly on the R-L Monitor System [the forerunner of P?S/8] on a configuration consisting of a 4K PDP-8 CPU, a TC01 and a single TU55 DECtape drive.  At the time, POLY BASIC only supported this configuration.\page The authors of POLY BASIC [Richard Lary and Lenny Elekman] became DEC employees and brought POLY BASIC with them to demonstrate to DEC management, who were very impressed with this standalone BASIC system.  They were "encouraged" to add on support for all then-current other storage devices; development was moved to PAL10 on an in-house TOPS-10 system.\line\line While the conceptual problem of creating POLY BASIC from a few binary modules parallels the same development issues for P?S/8 as described in this document, the authors were forced to reject the suggested method because the Typeset-8 group had influenced the maintainers of PAL10 to implement DTORG in an incompatible manner; binary paper-tapes produced by PAL10 cannot be loaded with the standard BIN loader.\line\line As this was unacceptable, they implemented a slightly more "guided" approach to the same overall method of system generation, even though it was obvious the alternative method described here would be advantageous.  The net result is a very long binary paper-tape set that used the BIN loader for all operations, but still requires a central system generation program; the modules cannot target the intended storage blocks.\par
\par
At some time later, DEC decided to co-opt POLY BASIC and revamp it slightly into EDU-System 30 [and later EDU-System 15/30 for the TD8E only].  Because of the additional overhead of a hamstrung development method, DEC assigned fledging programmers to finish what should have been a simple modification; in the end Richard Lary had to help the new programmers bring the product[s] to functioning condition.\par
\par
DEC management clearly had an opportunity to make decisions about how to modify the prevailing assembler for use with more active products than the stagnant Typeset-8.  Unfortunately, when OS/8 was being developed, no changes were made; OS/8 PAL8 just implemented DTORG identically to PAL10 to the benefit of no one.\line\line When OS/8 [or actually one of its predecessors] were in the early design stages, the binary file format was deliberately made far more inefficient than necessary merely to slavishly continue this poor design mechanism.\par
\par
It is clear that the far more efficient \i Slurp\i0  format was known to all parties involved at the time; however Slurp format was rejected in favor of placating the Typeset-8 group's demands without ever thinking through how all parties could be accommodated without needless overhead that effectively locks out program development of another block-writing utility unless completely compatible with the limitations of Typeset-8.\page Very early in the overall design period of OS/8, the author of this document suggested a practical alternative implementation of DTORG.  However, it was never considered and he was never a part of the design team.  [Note: He was asked to join the development group, but rejected DEC's offer of employment.  Instead, he chose to develop the R-L Monitor System along with some colleagues; this lead to the current implementation of P?S/8.  It is noteworthy that at least four other renowned PDP-8 experts, all employees of DEC at the time, also rejected the project that would eventually be known as OS/8.]\par
\par
Note: Several other programs have been written using analogous design principles, most notably OS/8 \i BUILD\i0 .  BUILD imparts special information within a compatible binary file using binary constructs that are vaguely analogous to the suggested mechanism to implement DTORG.\par
\par
2. Compatible implementation of DTORG directive support.\line\line By clever design, it is possible to maintain the integrity of the binary file format for all purposes whether the DTORG directive is involved or not.\par
\par
Note: With the burden of incompatible frames lifted, it would have been possible to implement Slurp format in OS/8; had this been accomplished, all binary loading operations for all users over the years could have been carried out in far less time.  Slurp format was demonstrated by the author to various DEC employees; binary file loading time using Slurp format is profoundly faster that any file format derived from paper-tape frames such as OS/8's .BN files; a DECtape-based P?S/8 system actually loaded large sample binary files \b faster\b0  than an RK05-based OS/8 system despite the large difference in basic data transfer rate of the two storage devices.\par
\par
A few of the design principles of BUILD were adapted to create compatible DTORG output yet maintain overall binary compatibility with all known loading programs.\par
\par
This is accomplished by the use of special origin settings in the file without any corresponding data word loading.  The sequence of origin settings is meaningless to the standard binary loaders [which handle the binary file correctly as long as the standard BIN checksum is correct within the file].  However, a special program aware of the presence of the origin groups can implement the intent of the DTORG directive.\line\line Note: If the Typeset-8 group remained unmovable and insisted their demands be met, this could still be done in a way that only affected their interests without harming that of all other users.  A conversion program [perhaps another option to OS/8 \i PIP\i0 ] could convert compatible binary files to Typeset-8 compliant binary paper-tapes].  Had that been done, P?S/8 BINCON would be largely unnecessary.\page The following PAL coding is the equivalent of what the DTORG directive does [to be released after the next major edit of P?S/8 PAL]:\par
\par
\f2 /       DTORG   DTBLOCK         /SOURCE CODE PROCESSED BELOW.\par
\par
        DTBLOCK=XXXX            /BLOCK EVALUATED FROM DTORG STATEMENT.\par
\par
        FOO=    .               /WE NEED THE CURRENT ORIGIN SETTING.\par
\par
        *7777                   /START OF DTORG SECTION. NEVER USED IN\par
                                /PRACTICAL PDP-8 PROGRAMMING OUTSIDE\par
                                /OF PERHAPS THE BIN AND RIM LOADER.\par
        *DTBLOCK                /DTORG VALUE FOR SPECIAL LOADER.\par
\par
        *FOO                    /RESTORE THE PREVIOUS ORIGIN SETTING.\par
\par
Internally, P?S/8 PAL has access to all of this information; an internal routine returns the binary value of the passed block argument.  If the argument is not present, the returned value is 0.\par
The corresponding origin settings are added to the binary output file stream; for conventional loading purposes, there is no net change in the binary output.  As such, the historic method of development can function as intended.\par
\par
Only a special block-writing utility aware of the special meaning of the origin settings can make use of this added data.  Implementation of the corrected DTORG directive in P?S/8 PAL will require less than 10 additional words of PDP-8 code over the current incomplete routine.  [Currently, P?S/8 PAL acknowledges the DTORG directive by processing the argument, then otherwise ignoring the statement.]\par
\par
Note: The specification for a functional block-writing utility is outside of the scope of this document.  Once meaningful use of the DTORG directive is added to the various P?S/8 source files, a formal specification will be drawn up leading to implementation of the alternate system generation block-writing utility.\par
\par
End of Appendix B.\par
